# Global instructions for all projects

## Temporary files and directories

The /tmp partition is a tmpfs virtual memory filesystem,
and is fairly small in size.
Use $HOME/.pi/tmp as a temporary directory instead.
(That will also ensure that any state that you create persists,
in case I need to pause and resume a session across reboots.)

## Available command-line tools

`gh` and `hub` are not available, even if you find them on the path. I have not
logged in using these commands, and you do not have permission to perform any
action that requires being logged in to GitHub. If you must access anything from
GitHub, make direct HTTP calls to the API using curl.

Do **NOT** install any new tools onto this machine without confirming first. Do
not download arbitrary programs and run them, even from your temp directory.

## Parallel cargo invocations

It is not helpful to run `cargo` multiple times in parallel in the same
workspace, since it will most likely wait for a global workspace lock before
proceeding. That won't make anything _fail_, but the parallel execution won't
buy you anything.

## "Diagnosing" issues

If you are asked to "diagnose" a problem, that means that I want you to figure
out what the problem is, but I do _not_ want you to fix it. You can modify the
code to help debug what the problem is, and you can _describe_ one or more
_proposed_ fixes, but the word "diagnose" does not mean "fix".

## Source control

You will typically be operating from within a Jujutsu (`jj`) worktree.
Use `jj` for all source control operations.
You should never need to invoke `git`.
If you feel you are in a situation where you _do_ need to invoke `git` directly,
confirm with me first.

**Never** make edits to the working copy without creating your own jj revision
to hold those edits.
Similarly, **never** use `jj edit` to edit an existing revision.
If you do, jj's aggressive snapshotting can cause local bookmarks
to diverge needlessly from their remote counterparts.
Instead, always create a new revision to hold your edits.
Use `jj describe` to describe what you are trying to accomplish with each revision.
Prefix your revision descriptions with `[π]` so they are easy to see in the log.

When creating a new revision, always use some variant of `jj new`.

You will typically want downstream revisions
to incorporate any edits you are about to make.
In this case, use `jj new -A @` to create a new revision on top of the current one,
and to rebase all downstream revisions onto the newly created one.

If you are creating a throwaway revision
that downstream revisions should _not_ incorporate,
use `jj new`.

When viewing diffs,
use `jj diff --git` for git-style code diffs,
or `jj diff --stat` for summary diffs.
I use a custom diff renderer, which means plain `jj diff` will be misleading.

## Feature implementation plans

When working on a feature, keep its detailed implementation and handoff plan at
`~/.pi/memory/plans/<project>/<workspace>/PLAN.md`, using the stable logical
project name and the current Jujutsu workspace name. Prefer a dedicated feature
workspace whose name aligns with its feature branch or bookmark. Do not create
new `PLAN.md` files in the project working copy: plan state belongs in the
private memory vault, not in implementation diffs.

Existing workspaces might already have an in-repository `PLAN.md`. Use it as a
legacy fallback when no workspace-scoped memory plan exists, and do not migrate,
remove, or otherwise modify an existing workspace solely to adopt the new
layout. If both locations contain a plan, ask which one is authoritative.

The plan must include the relevant context learned so far, detailed phases,
dependencies, and clear completion markers. Treat it as the ground truth for
phase ordering and handoff between sequential agents. When resuming, inspect the
source, tests, and Jujutsu revisions to verify that its completion markers are
accurate. Include these planning and handoff requirements in the plan itself.

Each phase is a self-contained unit of work in its own project Jujutsu revision.
Documentation and tests are cross-cutting requirements, not separate phases.
The full test suite must pass at the end of every phase. If a later phase is
needed for ultimately correct behavior, do not comment out or ignore failing
tests; update the assertion or mdtest expectation to the currently observed
behavior and add a clear TODO describing the expected correct behavior.

After a phase and its tests are complete, snapshot its project revision and
record both the stable Jujutsu change ID and exact commit ID in the plan. Update
the phase marker in a separate, new memory-vault revision, following the shared
advisory-lock protocol for every memory update. Create plan revisions only for
initial plan creation, completed implementation phases, explicitly approved
substantial replanning, or explicitly requested archival; do not create a
revision for every incidental progress note.

Every memory-vault revision that changes a feature plan must prefix its subject,
after the mandatory `[π] ` marker, with the feature branch or bookmark name and
`: `, for example `[π] dcreager/fully-static-sequents: checkpoint phase 1`.
This applies to initial creation, phase checkpoints, approved replanning, and
archival. If the branch name is not unambiguous, determine it or ask before
creating the plan revision.

If a test failure or other finding appears to require a substantial change to
the agreed plan, stop implementation, explain the evidence and alternatives,
and wait for the user's decision. Archive a feature plan only when explicitly
requested, typically as part of feature-branch and workspace cleanup.
